# Independent Automated YouTube Live Streaming Setup Complete

I have built the backend service in your `Live Streaming` folder as a completely standalone broadcasting service (similar to OneStream).

## What Was Implemented

1. **Independent Schedule Config ([schedule.json](file:///Users/samarthsharma/Documents/Live%20Streaming/schedule.json))**: You now control the schedule directly in this folder. You define the exact video file, the time, and the class title. 
2. **YogSaathi API Integrator**: Whenever a video is scheduled to stream, this app will automatically send a POST request to your main YogSaathi backend (`/api/yogaClasses`) using an Admin token to create the class.
3. **YouTube Broadcaster Engine**: Interfaces with the YouTube API to generate a unique Live Broadcast for every scheduled session.
4. **Schedule Engine**: A cron job manager that dynamically triggers based on the times you define in [schedule.json](file:///Users/samarthsharma/Documents/Live%20Streaming/schedule.json).

## Required Setup Steps

Before you can turn this on, you need to configure the connection to both YouTube and YogSaathi.

### Step 1: Prepare [.env](file:///Users/samarthsharma/Documents/YogSaathi/yoga-backend/.env) file
1. Open [/Users/samarthsharma/Documents/Live Streaming/.env.example](file:///Users/samarthsharma/Documents/Live%20Streaming/.env.example) and rename it to [.env](file:///Users/samarthsharma/Documents/YogSaathi/yoga-backend/.env).
2. Ensure you paste your `ADMIN_JWT_TOKEN` (the bearer token you use to login to YogSaathi as an admin) into this [.env](file:///Users/samarthsharma/Documents/YogSaathi/yoga-backend/.env). This allows the streamer to generate classes.
3. Make sure `YOGSAATHI_API_URL` points to your active backend (e.g., `https://yogsaathi.com/api`).

### Step 2: Set up YouTube Data API
1. Go to the [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/).
2. Create a Project and enable **YouTube Data API v3**.
3. Go to **APIs & Services -> OAuth consent screen** and set it up as External, adding your YouTube channel email as a Test User.
4. Go to **Credentials -> Create Credentials -> OAuth Client ID -> Web application**.
5. Give it a name and under **Authorized redirect URIs**, add `http://localhost:3000/oauth2callback`. Click **Create**.
6. Copy the **Client ID** and **Client Secret** into your [.env](file:///Users/samarthsharma/Documents/YogSaathi/yoga-backend/.env) file for `YOUTUBE_CLIENT_ID` and `YOUTUBE_CLIENT_SECRET`.

### Step 3: Authorize Your Channel
1. In your terminal, navigate to `/Users/samarthsharma/Documents/Live Streaming`
2. Run `node youtube-auth.js`
3. A URL will be printed in your terminal. Open it in your browser and authorize the app.
4. The terminal will then print a `YOUTUBE_REFRESH_TOKEN`. Copy that into your `.env` file and save.

### Step 4: Add Videos & Configure Schedule
1. Place your pre-recorded `.mp4` video files into the `/Users/samarthsharma/Documents/Live Streaming/videos` folder.
2. Edit `/Users/samarthsharma/Documents/Live Streaming/schedule.json`. This is where you map **times** to **video files**. Example:
   ```json
   {
     "time": "06:00",
     "video": "morning_flow.mp4",
     "title": "Morning Vinyasa Yoga",
     "focusArea": "Flexibility & Energy",
     "isActive": true
   }
   ```
   *Only items with `"isActive": true` will actually run.*

### Step 5: Start the Scheduler via Docker

We've containerized the app using a stable Ubuntu image with native FFmpeg, ensuring it works reliably across any server. Start it in the background using Docker Compose:

```bash
cd "/Users/samarthsharma/Documents/Live Streaming"
docker compose up -d
```

To view logs and see if streams are starting successfully:
```bash
docker compose logs -f
```

## How It Works in Production
1. At 06:00, the Docker container looks at `schedule.json` and sees `morning_flow.mp4`.
2. It automatically creates a YouTube Live broadcast and gets a unique Stream Key.
3. It makes an API Call to `POST /api/yogaClasses` on your main YogSaathi backend, telling it "I am streaming Morning Vinyasa Yoga right now at this YouTube Link."
4. It launches the system `ffmpeg` inside the container to push your `.mp4` video file directly to YouTube's ingest server, injecting a silent audio track if your video doesn't have one (a strict YouTube requirement).
